{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KjVKidiWAkPYT8hPSZtFnP","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: 'Original framing holds at full strength. 38% instability is empirically\n2\t  robust, architecture stratification (plan_010) shows C% drops to 20-25% confirming\n3\t  confounding is controllable rather than fatal, and all four claims survive review.\n4\t  Dropped experiments (003a/b/c, 005, 006, 008) are reported as future work or appendix\n5\t  confirmations — their absence does not weaken the core narrative. plan_002 (RL robustness)\n6\t  serves as supplementary defense despite small N. plan_010 is the single most impactful\n7\t  remaining experiment: it transforms the \"31% baseline FPR\" objection into \"architecture-stratified\n8\t  DIF reduces spurious flags by ~30%, demonstrating the method is refinable.\" Paper\n9\t  reads as definitive characterization study.\n10\t\n11\t  '\n12\timagined_experiments:\n13\t- name: rl_robustness_validation\n14\t  plan_id: plan_002\n15\t  description: 'Validate DIF robustness to reasoning-RL models by adding 10-15 open-weight\n16\t    GRPO/PPO models to METABENCH temporal cohorts and comparing DIF flag stability\n17\t    + traditional detector degradation.\n18\t\n19\t    '\n20\t  expected_results:\n21\t    n_reasoning_rl_models: 13\n22\t    n_alignment_rl_models: 477\n23\t    dif_flag_jaccard_with_vs_without_rl: 0.88\n24\t    dif_retention_ratio_rl_added: 0.93\n25\t    tost_equivalence_p: 0.008\n26\t    mink_auroc_sft_only: 0.81\n27\t    mink_auroc_alignment_rl: 0.74\n28\t    mink_auroc_reasoning_rl: 0.52\n29\t    constat_auroc_sft_only: 0.77\n30\t    constat_auroc_reasoning_rl: 0.48\n31\t    delong_dif_vs_constat_p: 0.003\n32\t    bootstrap_ci_dif_auroc:\n33\t    - 0.87\n34\t    - 0.94\n35\t    bootstrap_ci_mink_auroc_rl:\n36\t    - 0.41\n37\t    - 0.63\n38\t    partial_exploitation_detection_boundary: 0.45\n39\t  result_rationale: 'In best tier, DIF''s group-level design absorbs RL models without\n40\t    degradation because DIF operates on item-level response patterns across cohorts\n41\t    (N≥80 total), not on per-model NLL signals. Adding 13 reasoning-RL models to a\n42\t    cohort of ~800 shifts aggregate response distributions negligibly (Jaccard 0.88).\n43\t    Traditional detectors rely on model-level NLL gaps or generation variance — both\n44\t    are directly contracted by PPO clipping (Wang et al.), so AUROC drops to near-chance\n45\t    (0.48-0.52) on reasoning-RL individuals. TOST confirms equivalence at α=0.05.\n46\t    The detection boundary at p=0.45 means DIF maintains power above exploitation\n47\t    rate 45%, covering the operationally relevant regime where contamination actually\n48\t    inflates scores. N=13 is still acknowledged as a limitation in the paper, but\n49\t    the directional signal is unambiguous.\n50\t\n51\t    '\n52\t  tables:\n53\t  - 'Table S1: DIF flag stability under cohort composition perturbation (SFT-only\n54\t    vs +alignment-RL vs +reasoning-RL)'\n55\t  - 'Table S2: Traditional detector AUROC by model training regime with bootstrap\n56\t    95% CIs and DeLong pairwise tests'\n57\t  figures_description:\n58\t  - 'Figure S1: AUROC comparison bar chart — DIF vs Min-K% vs ConStat across three\n59\t    training regimes, with 95% bootstrap CIs'\n60\t  - 'Figure S2: Detection power curve as function of exploitation rate p ∈ [0.4, 1.0]\n61\t    showing DIF detection boundary'\n62\t  analysis: 'Supplementary result supporting Claim 1. DIF''s group-level architecture\n63\t    is fundamentally orthogonal to the NLL-gap-contraction mechanism that defeats\n64\t    per-model detectors. The result does not make a strong standalone contribution\n65\t    (N=13 is small), but it pre-empts the reviewer concern \"does RL break DIF?\" with\n66\t    empirical evidence rather than theoretical argument. In best tier, this is Appendix\n67\t    material referenced in §4.1 with a one-paragraph summary in the main text.\n68\t\n69\t    '\n70\t- name: constat_perturbation_eval\n71\t  plan_id: plan_003a\n72\t  description: 'Independent evaluation of ConStat-style perturbation testing on MMLU:\n73\t    generate 200+ semantically equivalent reformulations, compare original vs reformulated\n74\t    performance, apply FDR-controlled detection.\n75\t\n76\t    '\n77\t  expected_results:\n78\t    n_items_reformulated: 240\n79\t    n_models_evaluated: 15\n80\t    paraphrase_semantic_similarity: 0.94\n81\t    constat_detection_rate_fdr010: 0.32\n82\t    constat_auroc_non_rl: 0.73\n83\t    constat_auroc_rl: 0.44\n84\t    correlation_constat_vs_dif_flags: 0.21\n85\t    effect_size_verbatim_contaminated: 0.85\n86\t    effect_size_paraphrase_contaminated: 0.31\n87\t  result_rationale: 'In best tier, ConStat works well on non-RL models where verbatim\n88\t    memorization produces large original-vs-reformulated accuracy gaps (effect size\n89\t    0.85). AUROC 0.73 is respectable but not exceptional because MMLU items vary in\n90\t    reformulation difficulty — some semantically equivalent versions change difficulty\n91\t    unintentionally. On RL models, PPO''s generalization smooths out original-vs-reformulated\n92\t    gaps (effect size 0.31, AUROC 0.44). Low correlation with DIF flags (r=0.21) confirms\n93\t    orthogonal detection axes — ConStat catches per-model verbatim memorization, DIF\n94\t    catches cohort-level differential functioning. This orthogonality is exactly what\n95\t    motivates the ensemble concept (plan_003c), though the ensemble itself is deferred\n96\t    to future work.\n97\t\n98\t    '\n99\t  tables:\n100\t  - 'Table (future work): ConStat detection rates by MMLU subject category'\n101\t  figures_description:\n102\t  - 'Figure (future work): Scatter plot of ConStat detection score vs DIF Δ_MH showing\n103\t    orthogonal axes'\n104\t  analysis: 'Dropped from paper per pivot decision. In best tier, these results would\n105\t    appear in a follow-up ensemble paper. For ICLR submission, ConStat is referenced\n106\t    in Related Work as a complementary per-model approach, with the orthogonality\n107\t    observation (r=0.21) cited as motivation for future multi-signal work. Not running\n108\t    this experiment does NOT weaken the current paper — the paper''s contribution\n109\t    is the characterization finding, not detection methodology.\n110\t\n111\t    '\n112\t- name: dvd_generation_variance_eval\n113\t  plan_id: plan_003b\n114\t  description: 'Independent evaluation of DVD generation variance on MMLU: temperature\n115\t    sampling on 10-15 models, compute variance scores, detect bimodal contamination\n116\t    signal.\n117\t\n118\t    '\n119\t  expected_results:\n120\t    n_models: 12\n121\t    n_items_scored: 2400\n122\t    responses_per_item: 50\n123\t    sampling_temperature: 0.8\n124\t    dvd_auroc_verbatim: 0.66\n125\t    dvd_auroc_variant: 0.58\n126\t    bimodal_detection_rate: 0.14\n127\t    dvd_vs_dif_correlation: 0.15\n128\t    gpu_hours_consumed: 65\n129\t  result_rationale: 'In best tier, DVD captures verbatim contamination via bimodal\n130\t    response distributions (memorized items show near-deterministic correct answers\n131\t    at any temperature). AUROC 0.66 for verbatim is moderate — real contamination\n132\t    produces noisier bimodality than theory predicts because many \"contaminated\" items\n133\t    are also genuinely easy. Variant contamination detection (AUROC 0.58) is weak\n134\t    because paraphrased memorization produces smooth variance, not bimodality. Very\n135\t    low correlation with DIF (r=0.15) again confirms orthogonality: DVD is per-model\n136\t    generation-level, DIF is group-level response-pattern-level. The 65 GPU-hours\n137\t    cost is justified only if building toward the ensemble (plan_003c).\n138\t\n139\t    '\n140\t  tables:\n141\t  - 'Table (future work): DVD bimodal detection rates by contamination type'\n142\t  figures_description:\n143\t  - 'Figure (future work): Histogram of generation variance scores showing bimodal\n144\t    vs unimodal items'\n145\t  analysis: 'Dropped from paper. DVD''s moderate performance and high GPU cost make\n146\t    it the weakest signal in the proposed ensemble. In best tier, this result confirms\n147\t    that single-signal per-model detectors have inherent limitations — further supporting\n148\t    the paper''s pivot away from detection toward characterization. Not running this\n149\t    saves 65 GPU-hours for higher-priority experiments.\n150\t\n151\t    '\n152\t- name: ensemble_fusion\n153\t  plan_id: plan_003c\n154\t  description: 'Fuse DIF + ConStat + DVD into multi-signal ensemble with majority\n155\t    voting and weighted fusion.\n156\t\n157\t    '\n158\t  expected_results:\n159\t    ensemble_2of3_tpr: 0.84\n160\t    ensemble_2of3_fpr: 0.07\n161\t    weighted_fusion_auroc: 0.89\n162\t    uniform_weight_auroc: 0.86\n163\t    cross_validated_weight_stability: 0.03\n164\t    dif_coverage_delta_ge14: true\n165\t    constat_coverage_non_rl_delta_ge06: true\n166\t    dvd_coverage_variant: false\n167\t    effective_coverage_union: 0.78\n168\t  result_rationale: 'In best tier, ensemble achieves what no single detector can:\n169\t    broad coverage across contamination types with controlled FPR. 2-of-3 voting drives\n170\t    FPR to 0.07 because false positives are uncorrelated across orthogonal detection\n171\t    axes (Jaccard <0.21 pairwise). TPR 0.84 reflects the union of coverage regions\n172\t    — DIF catches large differential effects, ConStat catches per-model verbatim on\n173\t    non-RL, DVD adds marginal bimodal signal. Weighted fusion (AUROC 0.89) outperforms\n174\t    uniform (0.86) modestly, and cross-validated weights are stable (σ=0.03), indicating\n175\t    the ensemble is not overfitting. DVD contributes least — 2-signal DIF+ConStat\n176\t    would achieve AUROC 0.87, suggesting DVD is dispensable.\n177\t\n178\t    '\n179\t  tables:\n180\t  - 'Table (future work): Ensemble coverage matrix — which contamination types each\n181\t    signal catches'\n182\t  - 'Table (future work): Cross-validated fusion weights with stability analysis'\n183\t  figures_description:\n184\t  - 'Figure (future work): Venn diagram of DIF/ConStat/DVD detection overlap'\n185\t  - 'Figure (future work): ROC curves for individual signals vs ensemble'\n186\t  analysis: 'Dropped from paper — explicitly listed as future work. In best tier,\n187\t    ensemble results would be strong enough for a standalone methods paper (AUROC\n188\t    0.89 with FPR 0.07). For the current characterization paper, the ensemble is referenced\n189\t    as a natural extension: \"Our DIF analysis reveals the characterization; combining\n190\t    DIF with per-model detectors (ConStat, DVD) into an ensemble is a natural next\n191\t    step for practical detection.\" This positions a follow-up paper without weakening\n192\t    the current submission.\n193\t\n194\t    '\n195\t- name: bifactor_irt_equating_pilot\n196\t  plan_id: plan_005\n197\t  description: 'Pilot bifactor IRT equating on MMLU: fit bifactor model (general +\n198\t    subject-specific factors), select anchor items, calibrate replacements via fixed-parameter\n199\t    calibration.\n200\t\n201\t    '\n202\t  expected_results:\n203\t    bifactor_rmsea: 0.038\n204\t    bifactor_cfi: 0.91\n205\t    bifactor_tli: 0.89\n206\t    general_factor_variance_explained: 0.42\n207\t    subject_factor_avg_variance: 0.18\n208\t    n_anchor_items_selected: 4200\n209\t    anchor_stability_criterion: no DIF across 3 temporal cohorts\n210\t    c_pct_after_equating: 33.5\n211\t    equated_rho_original_vs_replaced: 0.965\n212\t    calibration_method: fixed-parameter (Kim & Kolen 2019)\n213\t  result_rationale: 'In best tier, bifactor model fits reasonably (RMSEA 0.038 within\n214\t    acceptable range, CFI 0.91 borderline) confirming that MMLU has genuine multidimensional\n215\t    structure — a general ability factor explains 42% of variance, with subject-specific\n216\t    factors adding 18% average. However, equating does NOT solve the DIF problem:\n217\t    C% only drops from 38.1% (within-subject, plan_014) to 33.5% after anchoring —\n218\t    consistent with plan_014''s finding that subject-specific factors account for\n219\t    only ~2.4 pp of the instability. Equated score correlation ρ=0.965 meets the ≥0.95\n220\t    criterion, but this just confirms MMLU''s massive redundancy (already shown by\n221\t    plan_017''s ρ=0.997). The bifactor structure is real but doesn''t rescue item\n222\t    stability. This is actually a GOOD result for the paper: it confirms the \"empirical\n223\t    observation\" about structural constraints — even proper psychometric modeling\n224\t    cannot eliminate the 31%+ baseline.\n225\t\n226\t    '\n227\t  tables:\n228\t  - 'Table (appendix): Bifactor model fit indices across model subsamples'\n229\t  figures_description:\n230\t  - 'Figure (appendix): Scree plot showing general vs subject-specific eigenvalue\n231\t    structure'\n232\t  analysis: 'Killed in practice (plan_015 status: killed) after plan_014 showed within-subject\n233\t    C%=38.1%. In best tier, running this would confirm rather than contradict the\n234\t    pivot: the multidimensional structure is real but insufficient to explain item\n235\t    instability. This strengthens the \"empirical observation\" narrative — we tried\n236\t    three structural corrections (matched-ability, IRT θ-conditioning, bifactor),\n237\t    all failed to reduce baseline below 31%. The result is cited in §4.4 as evidence\n238\t    that higher-dimensional IRT is needed (future work), not that DIF is broken.\n239\t\n240\t    '\n241\t- name: item_generation_calibration\n242\t  plan_id: plan_006\n243\t  description: 'Generate replacement items for DIF-flagged MMLU questions using LLM\n244\t    generation with construct constraints, calibrate via student simulation.\n245\t\n246\t    '\n247\t  expected_results:\n248\t    n_items_generated: 500\n249\t    n_subjects_covered: 30\n250\t    prompt_difficulty_rho: 0.28\n251\t    student_sim_difficulty_rho: 0.71\n252\t    student_sim_discrimination_rho: 0.58\n253\t    holdout_panel_n_models: 50\n254\t    holdout_difficulty_rho: 0.64\n255\t    holdout_discrimination_rho: 0.49\n256\t    construct_coverage_jaccard: 0.82\n257\t    gpu_hours_consumed: 180\n258\t  result_rationale: 'In best tier, student simulation (arXiv 2601.09953) substantially\n259\t    outperforms prompt-based difficulty targeting (ρ=0.71 vs 0.28), consistent with\n260\t    the literature''s range (r=0.75-0.82 reported, we get 0.71 on MMLU''s diverse\n261\t    domains). Discrimination calibration is harder (ρ=0.58) because discrimination\n262\t    depends on item-ability interaction, not just surface difficulty. Holdout validation\n263\t    shows modest shrinkage (difficulty ρ drops from 0.71 to 0.64) due to distribution\n264\t    shift between student simulation and real model population. Construct coverage\n265\t    Jaccard 0.82 indicates generated items cover most of the original construct space\n266\t    but miss some domain-specific nuances (e.g., professional medicine clinical reasoning\n267\t    chains). This is a solid pilot but insufficient for operational replacement —\n268\t    the \"graceful degradation\" question (plan_008) remains.\n269\t\n270\t    '\n271\t  tables:\n272\t  - 'Table (future work): Difficulty and discrimination calibration accuracy by MMLU\n273\t    subject'\n274\t  figures_description:\n275\t  - 'Figure (future work): Scatter of predicted vs observed difficulty — student sim\n276\t    vs prompt-only'\n277\t  analysis: 'Dropped from paper (regeneration claim killed). In best tier, these results\n278\t    would anchor a follow-up paper on automated benchmark maintenance. For the current\n279\t    submission, item generation is mentioned in Future Work (§7) as a practical application\n280\t    of DIF diagnostics: \"DIF identifies which items need replacement; student simulation\n281\t    calibration provides a feasible generation pipeline (ρ=0.71 difficulty calibration\n282\t    in pilot).\" One sentence, no more — the characterization paper does not need to\n283\t    solve the replacement problem.\n284\t\n285\t    '\n286\t- name: cumulative_bayesian_dif\n287\t  plan_id: plan_007\n288\t  description: 'Simulate cumulative Bayesian DIF priors over 4 successive benchmark\n289\t    administrations, showing power improvement from evidence accumulation.\n290\t\n291\t    '\n292\t  expected_results:\n293\t    admin_1_tpr_delta10: 0.42\n294\t    admin_2_tpr_delta10: 0.58\n295\t    admin_3_tpr_delta10: 0.71\n296\t    admin_4_tpr_delta10: 0.78\n297\t    admin_1_fpr: 0.041\n298\t    admin_4_fpr: 0.039\n299\t    prior_posterior_shrinkage: 0.35\n300\t    bayes_factor_threshold: 3.2\n301\t    n_simulated_models_per_admin: 200\n302\t    delta_values_tested:\n303\t    - 0.8\n304\t    - 1.0\n305\t    - 1.2\n306\t    - 1.4\n307\t    power_at_delta08_admin4: 0.53\n308\t    power_at_delta14_admin4: 0.89\n309\t  result_rationale: 'In best tier, cumulative Bayesian priors provide the expected\n310\t    statistical benefit: each administration adds ~15 pp TPR for medium effects (δ=1.0),\n311\t    reaching 0.71 after 3 administrations (vs 0.42 single-shot). FPR remains stable\n312\t    at ~0.04 because Bayesian updating with proper priors is conservative on non-DIF\n313\t    items. The key insight is that DIF detection becomes operationally viable for\n314\t    medium effects (δ=0.8-1.2) through temporal accumulation — exactly the regime\n315\t    where single-shot MH testing lacks power (TPR<0.50). At δ=1.4 (the current operational\n316\t    threshold), power reaches 0.89 by administration 4, approaching ceiling. The Bayes\n317\t    factor threshold of 3.2 (\"substantial evidence\") provides a principled decision\n318\t    criterion superior to the arbitrary |Δ_MH|≥1.5 cutoff.\n319\t\n320\t    '\n321\t  tables:\n322\t  - 'Table (appendix): Cumulative power by administration × effect size matrix'\n323\t  figures_description:\n324\t  - 'Figure (appendix): Power accumulation curves over 4 administrations for δ ∈ {0.8,\n325\t    1.0, 1.2, 1.4}'\n326\t  analysis: 'Supplementary contribution supporting Claim 1''s practical utility. In\n327\t    best tier, this provides a forward-looking operational recommendation: \"Benchmark\n328\t    maintainers should accumulate DIF evidence across evaluation rounds rather than\n329\t    relying on single-shot detection.\" This transitions the paper from retrospective\n330\t    characterization to prospective methodology. Appears as §5.3 or Appendix B, ~1\n331\t    page. Does not change the core claims but adds practical value that reviewers\n332\t    appreciate for a characterization paper.\n333\t\n334\t    '\n335\t- name: graceful_degradation_analysis\n336\t  plan_id: plan_008\n337\t  description: 'Formal analysis of benchmark validity under progressive item replacement\n338\t    (5%-50%), establishing theoretical bounds on validity degradation.\n339\t\n340\t    '\n341\t  expected_results:\n342\t    rho_at_5pct_replacement: 0.9991\n343\t    rho_at_10pct_replacement: 0.9978\n344\t    rho_at_20pct_replacement: 0.9943\n345\t    rho_at_50pct_replacement: 0.9821\n346\t    shr_at_20pct_replacement: 0.988\n347\t    construct_coverage_at_20pct: 0.96\n348\t    construct_coverage_at_50pct: 0.88\n349\t    theoretical_lower_bound_rho: 1 - 2r(1-r) where r=replacement fraction\n350\t    empirical_vs_theoretical_gap: 0.008\n351\t  result_rationale: 'In best tier, MMLU''s massive redundancy (plan_017 showed ρ=0.997\n352\t    after 31% removal) means progressive replacement barely degrades rankings. Even\n353\t    at 50% replacement, ρ=0.982 because the remaining 50% of items carry sufficient\n354\t    discriminative information — MMLU has ~3× more items than needed for stable rankings.\n355\t    Construct coverage degrades faster (0.88 at 50%) because some subjects have few\n356\t    items, and replacing them risks changing the construct space. The theoretical\n357\t    bound 1-2r(1-r) provides a model-free prediction that tracks empirical results\n358\t    within 0.008, giving benchmark maintainers a planning tool. The key finding: MMLU\n359\t    can tolerate aggressive item replacement (up to 20%) with negligible validity\n360\t    loss, confirming that DIF-flagged items CAN be replaced without breaking the benchmark.\n361\t\n362\t    '\n363\t  tables:\n364\t  - 'Table (future work): Validity metrics × replacement rate matrix with theoretical\n365\t    bounds'\n366\t  figures_description:\n367\t  - 'Figure (future work): Degradation curve — Spearman ρ and construct coverage vs\n368\t    replacement rate'\n369\t  analysis: 'Dropped (depends on plan_005/006 which were killed). In best tier, this\n370\t    result would be a strong practical contribution — proving MMLU is replaceable\n371\t    at scale. For the current paper, plan_017''s ρ=0.997 result already makes the\n372\t    key point about redundancy. Graceful degradation is cited in Future Work: \"The\n373\t    massive redundancy we observe (ρ=0.997 after 31% item removal) suggests benchmark\n374\t    maintenance via item replacement is feasible; formal degradation analysis is underway.\"\n375\t\n376\t    '\n377\t- name: item_focused_trees\n378\t  plan_id: plan_009\n379\t  description: 'Implement Item-Focused Trees (Tutz & Berger 2016) treating model release\n380\t    date as continuous covariate, comparing detection power and flexibility vs categorical\n381\t    DIF.\n382\t\n383\t    '\n384\t  expected_results:\n385\t    n_items_analyzed: 12508\n386\t    continuous_covariate: release_date (days since 2023-01-01)\n387\t    ift_c_pct_equivalent: 28.4\n388\t    ift_vs_categorical_flag_jaccard: 0.76\n389\t    ift_unique_flags: 412\n390\t    categorical_unique_flags: 891\n391\t    ift_auc_semi_synthetic: 0.91\n392\t    categorical_auc_semi_synthetic: 0.88\n393\t    ift_smooth_transition_detected: 0.34\n394\t    tree_depth_median: 3\n395\t    split_points_cluster_around: 2023-Q4 and 2024-Q2\n396\t  result_rationale: 'In best tier, Item-Focused Trees capture gradual DIF transitions\n397\t    that categorical splits miss. IFT identifies 28.4% of items as functionally unstable\n398\t    (vs 31.3% categorical) — slightly fewer because continuous modeling avoids the\n399\t    artificial inflation from forcing a binary cohort split. Jaccard 0.76 with categorical\n400\t    flags shows substantial overlap but 412 unique IFT detections capture items with\n401\t    smooth temporal drift (34% show gradual transition patterns). AUC 0.91 on semi-synthetic\n402\t    slightly outperforms categorical (0.88) because IFT''s flexible splits adapt to\n403\t    the actual contamination timing. Tree split points cluster around 2023-Q4 and\n404\t    2024-Q2, aligning with major model release waves (Llama-2→3 transition, Qwen-1.5→2).\n405\t    This provides temporal resolution that categorical DIF cannot.\n406\t\n407\t    '\n408\t  tables:\n409\t  - 'Table (appendix): Item-Focused Tree vs categorical DIF detection concordance'\n410\t  figures_description:\n411\t  - 'Figure (appendix): Example IFT tree for a single MMLU item showing DIF trajectory\n412\t    over release date'\n413\t  - 'Figure (appendix): Split-point histogram showing clustering around model release\n414\t    waves'\n415\t  analysis: 'P2 supplementary analysis. In best tier, IFT adds methodological sophistication\n416\t    without changing the core finding — 28-31% of items are unstable regardless of\n417\t    detection method. The temporal resolution (split points at model release waves)\n418\t    provides interpretive value: instability is concentrated at model generation transitions,\n419\t    consistent with the capability- improvement interpretation (ρ=0.307 from plan_001).\n420\t    Appears as Appendix C, ~0.5 pages. Strengthens Claim 1 by showing the 38% finding\n421\t    is robust to methodological variation.\n422\t\n423\t    '\n424\t- name: architecture_stratified_dif\n425\t  plan_id: plan_010\n426\t  description: 'Run DIF within decoder-only models only to test whether architecture\n427\t    homogeneity reduces spurious DIF and improves IRT model fit.\n428\t\n429\t    '\n430\t  expected_results:\n431\t    n_decoder_only_total: 3847\n432\t    n_decoder_only_ref: 892\n433\t    n_decoder_only_foc: 541\n434\t    decoder_only_c_pct: 22.1\n435\t    full_population_c_pct: 31.3\n436\t    c_pct_reduction: 29.4%\n437\t    irt_rmsea_full: 0.082\n438\t    irt_rmsea_decoder_only: 0.037\n439\t    irt_cfi_decoder_only: 0.93\n440\t    irt_tli_decoder_only: 0.91\n441\t    irt_srmr_decoder_only: 0.028\n442\t    semi_synthetic_delta_tpr_decoder_only: 0.72\n443\t    semi_synthetic_delta_tpr_full: 0.674\n444\t    semi_synthetic_fpr_decoder_only: 0.221\n445\t    semi_synthetic_fpr_full: 0.314\n446\t    decoder_only_sanity_random_split_c_pct: 0.12\n447\t    dif_flag_overlap_full_vs_decoder_only: 0.68\n448\t    enrichment_vs_li_decoder_only: 0.98\n449\t  result_rationale: 'In best tier, restricting to decoder-only models removes architecture\n450\t    as a confounding factor — the unidimensional IRT assumption is more tenable when\n451\t    all models share the same basic architecture (autoregressive transformer). RMSEA\n452\t    drops from 0.082 (poor fit) to 0.037 (acceptable fit), confirming that architecture\n453\t    heterogeneity was driving model misfit, not the items themselves. C% drops from\n454\t    31.3% to 22.1% — a 29% reduction — meaning ~9 pp of the original flags were architecture-confounded\n455\t    (encoder-decoder and mixture-of-experts models process items differently, creating\n456\t    artificial DIF). The remaining 22.1% represents genuine temporal instability within\n457\t    a homogeneous architecture population. Semi-synthetic validation improves: FPR\n458\t    drops from 0.314 to 0.221 (fewer false alarms), ΔTPR increases from 0.674 to 0.72\n459\t    (better signal-to-noise). Sanity check confirms the reduction is not from power\n460\t    loss (random split C% stays at 0.12%). Flag overlap of 0.68 with full-population\n461\t    analysis shows the core DIF items persist — architecture stratification refines\n462\t    rather than contradicts the original finding.\n463\t\n464\t    '\n465\t  tables:\n466\t  - 'Table 4: IRT fit diagnostics — full population vs decoder-only subgroup'\n467\t  - 'Table 5: DIF detection rates and semi-synthetic validation — architecture-stratified\n468\t    vs full'\n469\t  figures_description:\n470\t  - 'Figure 5: C% comparison bar chart — full population (31.3%) vs decoder-only (22.1%)\n471\t    vs random sanity (0.12%)'\n472\t  - 'Figure 6: IRT fit improvement — RMSEA/CFI/TLI comparison showing acceptable fit\n473\t    in homogeneous subgroup'\n474\t  analysis: 'CRITICAL experiment for best tier — transforms the strongest reviewer\n475\t    objection into a methodological contribution. The finding is dual: (1) architecture\n476\t    heterogeneity inflates DIF by ~9 pp, confirming the concern is real; (2) 22.1%\n477\t    genuine instability persists even in a homogeneous population, confirming the\n478\t    core finding is NOT an artifact. The practical recommendation — \"run DIF within\n479\t    architecture-homogeneous subpopulations\" — is simple, zero-cost, and immediately\n480\t    actionable. This appears as §4.3 in the main text (~1.5 pages) and is referenced\n481\t    in the abstract. In best tier, reviewers read this as: \"the authors anticipated\n482\t    our concern, ran the experiment, showed the method is refinable, and the core\n483\t    finding survives.\" This is the difference between a borderline accept and a clear\n484\t    accept. IRT RMSEA 0.037 falls within the best-tier target range [0.02, 0.05],\n485\t    and C% 22.1% is within [18, 25]. The architecture stratification narrative complements\n486\t    Growing Pains: their anchor-item approach also benefits from architecture-homogeneous\n487\t    calibration.'\n488\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bed74537-8027-4ea2-ace4-14fc8a5d1a29","uuid":"2634e5e3-90e8-4b56-8fa9-17d468505060","timestamp":"2026-05-21T01:58:33.107Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T012912/best/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: 'Original framing holds at full strength. 38% instability is empirically\n  robust, architecture stratification (plan_010) shows C% drops to 20-25% confirming\n  confounding is controllable rather than fatal, and all four claims survive review.\n  Dropped experiments (003a/b/c, 005, 006, 008) are reported as future work or appendix\n  confirmations — their absence does not weaken the core narrative. plan_002 (RL robustness)\n  serves as supplementary defense despite small N. plan_010 is the single most impactful\n  remaining experiment: it transforms the \"31% baseline FPR\" objection into \"architecture-stratified\n  DIF reduces spurious flags by ~30%, demonstrating the method is refinable.\" Paper\n  reads as definitive characterization study.\n\n  '\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: rl_robustness_validation\n  plan_id: plan_002\n  description: 'Validate DIF robustness to reasoning-RL models by adding 10-15 open-weight\n    GRPO/PPO models to METABENCH temporal cohorts and comparing DIF flag stability\n    + traditional detector degradation.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    n_reasoning_rl_models: 13\n    n_alignment_rl_models: 477\n    dif_flag_jaccard_with_vs_without_rl: 0.88\n    dif_retention_ratio_rl_added: 0.93\n    tost_equivalence_p: 0.008\n    mink_auroc_sft_only: 0.81\n    mink_auroc_alignment_rl: 0.74\n    mink_auroc_reasoning_rl: 0.52\n    constat_auroc_sft_only: 0.77\n    constat_auroc_reasoning_rl: 0.48\n    delong_dif_vs_constat_p: 0.003\n    bootstrap_ci_dif_auroc:\n    - 0.87\n    - 0.94\n    bootstrap_ci_mink_auroc_rl:\n    - 0.41\n    - 0.63\n    partial_exploitation_detection_boundary: 0.45\n  result_rationale: 'In best tier, DIF''s group-level design absorbs RL models without\n    degradation because DIF operates on item-level response patterns across cohorts\n    (N≥80 total), not on per-model NLL signals. Adding 13 reasoning-RL models to a\n    cohort of ~800 shifts aggregate response distributions negligibly (Jaccard 0.88).\n    Traditional detectors rely on model-level NLL gaps or generation variance — both\n    are directly contracted by PPO clipping (Wang et al.), so AUROC drops to near-chance\n    (0.48-0.52) on reasoning-RL individuals. TOST confirms equivalence at α=0.05.\n    The detection boundary at p=0.45 means DIF maintains power above exploitation\n    rate 45%, covering the operationally relevant regime where contamination actually\n    inflates scores. N=13 is still acknowledged as a limitation in the paper, but\n    the directional signal is unambiguous.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - 'Table S1: DIF flag stability under cohort composition perturbation (SFT-only\n    vs +alignment-RL vs +reasoning-RL)'\n  - 'Table S2: Traditional detector AUROC by model training regime with bootstrap\n    95% CIs and DeLong pairwise tests'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure S1: AUROC comparison bar chart — DIF vs Min-K% vs ConStat across three\n    training regimes, with 95% bootstrap CIs'\n  - 'Figure S2: Detection power curve as function of exploitation rate p ∈ [0.4, 1.0]\n    showing DIF detection boundary'\n  analysis: 'Supplementary result supporting Claim 1. DIF''s group-level architecture\n    is fundamentally orthogonal to the NLL-gap-contraction mechanism that defeats\n    per-model detectors. The result does not make a strong standalone contribution\n    (N=13 is small), but it pre-empts the reviewer concern \"does RL break DIF?\" with\n    empirical evidence rather than theoretical argument. In best tier, this is Appendix\n    material referenced in §4.1 with a one-paragraph summary in the main text.\n\n    '\n- name: constat_perturbation_eval\n  plan_id: plan_003a\n  description: 'Independent evaluation of ConStat-style perturbation testing on MMLU:\n    generate 200+ semantically equivalent reformulations, compare original vs reformulated\n    performance, apply FDR-controlled detection.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    n_items_reformulated: 240\n    n_models_evaluated: 15\n    paraphrase_semantic_similarity: 0.94\n    constat_detection_rate_fdr010: 0.32\n    constat_auroc_non_rl: 0.73\n    constat_auroc_rl: 0.44\n    correlation_constat_vs_dif_flags: 0.21\n    effect_size_verbatim_contaminated: 0.85\n    effect_size_paraphrase_contaminated: 0.31\n  result_rationale: 'In best tier, ConStat works well on non-RL models where verbatim\n    memorization produces large original-vs-reformulated accuracy gaps (effect size\n    0.85). AUROC 0.73 is respectable but not exceptional because MMLU items vary in\n    reformulation difficulty — some semantically equivalent versions change difficulty\n    unintentionally. On RL models, PPO''s generalization smooths out original-vs-reformulated\n    gaps (effect size 0.31, AUROC 0.44). Low correlation with DIF flags (r=0.21) confirms\n    orthogonal detection axes — ConStat catches per-model verbatim memorization, DIF\n    catches cohort-level differential functioning. This orthogonality is exactly what\n    motivates the ensemble concept (plan_003c), though the ensemble itself is deferred\n    to future work.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - 'Table (future work): ConStat detection rates by MMLU subject category'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure (future work): Scatter plot of ConStat detection score vs DIF Δ_MH showing\n    orthogonal axes'\n  analysis: 'Dropped from paper per pivot decision. In best tier, these results would\n    appear in a follow-up ensemble paper. For ICLR submission, ConStat is referenced\n    in Related Work as a complementary per-model approach, with the orthogonality\n    observation (r=0.21) cited as motivation for future multi-signal work. Not running\n    this experiment does NOT weaken the current paper — the paper''s contribution\n    is the characterization finding, not detection methodology.\n\n    '\n- name: dvd_generation_variance_eval\n  plan_id: plan_003b\n  description: 'Independent evaluation of DVD generation variance on MMLU: temperature\n    sampling on 10-15 models, compute variance scores, detect bimodal contamination\n    signal.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    n_models: 12\n    n_items_scored: 2400\n    responses_per_item: 50\n    sampling_temperature: 0.8\n    dvd_auroc_verbatim: 0.66\n    dvd_auroc_variant: 0.58\n    bimodal_detection_rate: 0.14\n    dvd_vs_dif_correlation: 0.15\n    gpu_hours_consumed: 65\n  result_rationale: 'In best tier, DVD captures verbatim contamination via bimodal\n    response distributions (memorized items show near-deterministic correct answers\n    at any temperature). AUROC 0.66 for verbatim is moderate — real contamination\n    produces noisier bimodality than theory predicts because many \"contaminated\" items\n    are also genuinely easy. Variant contamination detection (AUROC 0.58) is weak\n    because paraphrased memorization produces smooth variance, not bimodality. Very\n    low correlation with DIF (r=0.15) again confirms orthogonality: DVD is per-model\n    generation-level, DIF is group-level response-pattern-level. The 65 GPU-hours\n    cost is justified only if building toward the ensemble (plan_003c).\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - 'Table (future work): DVD bimodal detection rates by contamination type'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure (future work): Histogram of generation variance scores showing bimodal\n    vs unimodal items'\n  analysis: 'Dropped from paper. DVD''s moderate performance and high GPU cost make\n    it the weakest signal in the proposed ensemble. In best tier, this result confirms\n    that single-signal per-model detectors have inherent limitations — further supporting\n    the paper''s pivot away from detection toward characterization. Not running this\n    saves 65 GPU-hours for higher-priority experiments.\n\n    '\n- name: ensemble_fusion\n  plan_id: plan_003c\n  description: 'Fuse DIF + ConStat + DVD into multi-signal ensemble with majority\n    voting and weighted fusion.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    ensemble_2of3_tpr: 0.84\n    ensemble_2of3_fpr: 0.07\n    weighted_fusion_auroc: 0.89\n    uniform_weight_auroc: 0.86\n    cross_validated_weight_stability: 0.03\n    dif_coverage_delta_ge14: true\n    constat_coverage_non_rl_delta_ge06: true\n    dvd_coverage_variant: false\n    effective_coverage_union: 0.78\n  result_rationale: 'In best tier, ensemble achieves what no single detector can:\n    broad coverage across contamination types with controlled FPR. 2-of-3 voting drives\n    FPR to 0.07 because false positives are uncorrelated across orthogonal detection\n    axes (Jaccard <0.21 pairwise). TPR 0.84 reflects the union of coverage regions\n    — DIF catches large differential effects, ConStat catches per-model verbatim on\n    non-RL, DVD adds marginal bimodal signal. Weighted fusion (AUROC 0.89) outperforms\n    uniform (0.86) modestly, and cross-validated weights are stable (σ=0.03), indicating\n    the ensemble is not overfitting. DVD contributes least — 2-signal DIF+ConStat\n    would achieve AUROC 0.87, suggesting DVD is dispensable.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - 'Table (future work): Ensemble coverage matrix — which contamination types each\n    signal catches'\n  - 'Table (future work): Cross-validated fusion weights with stability analysis'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure (future work): Venn diagram of DIF/ConStat/DVD detection overlap'\n  - 'Figure (future work): ROC curves for individual signals vs ensemble'\n  analysis: 'Dropped from paper — explicitly listed as future work. In best tier,\n    ensemble results would be strong enough for a standalone methods paper (AUROC\n    0.89 with FPR 0.07). For the current characterization paper, the ensemble is referenced\n    as a natural extension: \"Our DIF analysis reveals the characterization; combining\n    DIF with per-model detectors (ConStat, DVD) into an ensemble is a natural next\n    step for practical detection.\" This positions a follow-up paper without weakening\n    the current submission.\n\n    '\n- name: bifactor_irt_equating_pilot\n  plan_id: plan_005\n  description: 'Pilot bifactor IRT equating on MMLU: fit bifactor model (general +\n    subject-specific factors), select anchor items, calibrate replacements via fixed-parameter\n    calibration.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    bifactor_rmsea: 0.038\n    bifactor_cfi: 0.91\n    bifactor_tli: 0.89\n    general_factor_variance_explained: 0.42\n    subject_factor_avg_variance: 0.18\n    n_anchor_items_selected: 4200\n    anchor_stability_criterion: no DIF across 3 temporal cohorts\n    c_pct_after_equating: 33.5\n    equated_rho_original_vs_replaced: 0.965\n    calibration_method: fixed-parameter (Kim & Kolen 2019)\n  result_rationale: 'In best tier, bifactor model fits reasonably (RMSEA 0.038 within\n    acceptable range, CFI 0.91 borderline) confirming that MMLU has genuine multidimensional\n    structure — a general ability factor explains 42% of variance, with subject-specific\n    factors adding 18% average. However, equating does NOT solve the DIF problem:\n    C% only drops from 38.1% (within-subject, plan_014) to 33.5% after anchoring —\n    consistent with plan_014''s finding that subject-specific factors account for\n    only ~2.4 pp of the instability. Equated score correlation ρ=0.965 meets the ≥0.95\n    criterion, but this just confirms MMLU''s massive redundancy (already shown by\n    plan_017''s ρ=0.997). The bifactor structure is real but doesn''t rescue item\n    stability. This is actually a GOOD result for the paper: it confirms the \"empirical\n    observation\" about structural constraints — even proper psychometric modeling\n    cannot eliminate the 31%+ baseline.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - 'Table (appendix): Bifactor model fit indices across model subsamples'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure (appendix): Scree plot showing general vs subject-specific eigenvalue\n    structure'\n  analysis: 'Killed in practice (plan_015 status: killed) after plan_014 showed within-subject\n    C%=38.1%. In best tier, running this would confirm rather than contradict the\n    pivot: the multidimensional structure is real but insufficient to explain item\n    instability. This strengthens the \"empirical observation\" narrative — we tried\n    three structural corrections (matched-ability, IRT θ-conditioning, bifactor),\n    all failed to reduce baseline below 31%. The result is cited in §4.4 as evidence\n    that higher-dimensional IRT is needed (future work), not that DIF is broken.\n\n    '\n- name: item_generation_calibration\n  plan_id: plan_006\n  description: 'Generate replacement items for DIF-flagged MMLU questions using LLM\n    generation with construct constraints, calibrate via student simulation.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    n_items_generated: 500\n    n_subjects_covered: 30\n    prompt_difficulty_rho: 0.28\n    student_sim_difficulty_rho: 0.71\n    student_sim_discrimination_rho: 0.58\n    holdout_panel_n_models: 50\n    holdout_difficulty_rho: 0.64\n    holdout_discrimination_rho: 0.49\n    construct_coverage_jaccard: 0.82\n    gpu_hours_consumed: 180\n  result_rationale: 'In best tier, student simulation (arXiv 2601.09953) substantially\n    outperforms prompt-based difficulty targeting (ρ=0.71 vs 0.28), consistent with\n    the literature''s range (r=0.75-0.82 reported, we get 0.71 on MMLU''s diverse\n    domains). Discrimination calibration is harder (ρ=0.58) because discrimination\n    depends on item-ability interaction, not just surface difficulty. Holdout validation\n    shows modest shrinkage (difficulty ρ drops from 0.71 to 0.64) due to distribution\n    shift between student simulation and real model population. Construct coverage\n    Jaccard 0.82 indicates generated items cover most of the original construct space\n    but miss some domain-specific nuances (e.g., professional medicine clinical reasoning\n    chains). This is a solid pilot but insufficient for operational replacement —\n    the \"graceful degradation\" question (plan_008) remains.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - 'Table (future work): Difficulty and discrimination calibration accuracy by MMLU\n    subject'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure (future work): Scatter of predicted vs observed difficulty — student sim\n    vs prompt-only'\n  analysis: 'Dropped from paper (regeneration claim killed). In best tier, these results\n    would anchor a follow-up paper on automated benchmark maintenance. For the current\n    submission, item generation is mentioned in Future Work (§7) as a practical application\n    of DIF diagnostics: \"DIF identifies which items need replacement; student simulation\n    calibration provides a feasible generation pipeline (ρ=0.71 difficulty calibration\n    in pilot).\" One sentence, no more — the characterization paper does not need to\n    solve the replacement problem.\n\n    '\n- name: cumulative_bayesian_dif\n  plan_id: plan_007\n  description: 'Simulate cumulative Bayesian DIF priors over 4 successive benchmark\n    administrations, showing power improvement from evidence accumulation.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    admin_1_tpr_delta10: 0.42\n    admin_2_tpr_delta10: 0.58\n    admin_3_tpr_delta10: 0.71\n    admin_4_tpr_delta10: 0.78\n    admin_1_fpr: 0.041\n    admin_4_fpr: 0.039\n    prior_posterior_shrinkage: 0.35\n    bayes_factor_threshold: 3.2\n    n_simulated_models_per_admin: 200\n    delta_values_tested:\n    - 0.8\n    - 1.0\n    - 1.2\n    - 1.4\n    power_at_delta08_admin4: 0.53\n    power_at_delta14_admin4: 0.89\n  result_rationale: 'In best tier, cumulative Bayesian priors provide the expected\n    statistical benefit: each administration adds ~15 pp TPR for medium effects (δ=1.0),\n    reaching 0.71 after 3 administrations (vs 0.42 single-shot). FPR remains stable\n    at ~0.04 because Bayesian updating with proper priors is conservative on non-DIF\n    items. The key insight is that DIF detection becomes operationally viable for\n    medium effects (δ=0.8-1.2) through temporal accumulation — exactly the regime\n    where single-shot MH testing lacks power (TPR<0.50). At δ=1.4 (the current operational\n    threshold), power reaches 0.89 by administration 4, approaching ceiling. The Bayes\n    factor threshold of 3.2 (\"substantial evidence\") provides a principled decision\n    criterion superior to the arbitrary |Δ_MH|≥1.5 cutoff.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - 'Table (appendix): Cumulative power by administration × effect size matrix'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure (appendix): Power accumulation curves over 4 administrations for δ ∈ {0.8,\n    1.0, 1.2, 1.4}'\n  analysis: 'Supplementary contribution supporting Claim 1''s practical utility. In\n    best tier, this provides a forward-looking operational recommendation: \"Benchmark\n    maintainers should accumulate DIF evidence across evaluation rounds rather than\n    relying on single-shot detection.\" This transitions the paper from retrospective\n    characterization to prospective methodology. Appears as §5.3 or Appendix B, ~1\n    page. Does not change the core claims but adds practical value that reviewers\n    appreciate for a characterization paper.\n\n    '\n- name: graceful_degradation_analysis\n  plan_id: plan_008\n  description: 'Formal analysis of benchmark validity under progressive item replacement\n    (5%-50%), establishing theoretical bounds on validity degradation.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    rho_at_5pct_replacement: 0.9991\n    rho_at_10pct_replacement: 0.9978\n    rho_at_20pct_replacement: 0.9943\n    rho_at_50pct_replacement: 0.9821\n    shr_at_20pct_replacement: 0.988\n    construct_coverage_at_20pct: 0.96\n    construct_coverage_at_50pct: 0.88\n    theoretical_lower_bound_rho: 1 - 2r(1-r) where r=replacement fraction\n    empirical_vs_theoretical_gap: 0.008\n  result_rationale: 'In best tier, MMLU''s massive redundancy (plan_017 showed ρ=0.997\n    after 31% removal) means progressive replacement barely degrades rankings. Even\n    at 50% replacement, ρ=0.982 because the remaining 50% of items carry sufficient\n    discriminative information — MMLU has ~3× more items than needed for stable rankings.\n    Construct coverage degrades faster (0.88 at 50%) because some subjects have few\n    items, and replacing them risks changing the construct space. The theoretical\n    bound 1-2r(1-r) provides a model-free prediction that tracks empirical results\n    within 0.008, giving benchmark maintainers a planning tool. The key finding: MMLU\n    can tolerate aggressive item replacement (up to 20%) with negligible validity\n    loss, confirming that DIF-flagged items CAN be replaced without breaking the benchmark.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - 'Table (future work): Validity metrics × replacement rate matrix with theoretical\n    bounds'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure (future work): Degradation curve — Spearman ρ and construct coverage vs\n    replacement rate'\n  analysis: 'Dropped (depends on plan_005/006 which were killed). In best tier, this\n    result would be a strong practical contribution — proving MMLU is replaceable\n    at scale. For the current paper, plan_017''s ρ=0.997 result already makes the\n    key point about redundancy. Graceful degradation is cited in Future Work: \"The\n    massive redundancy we observe (ρ=0.997 after 31% item removal) suggests benchmark\n    maintenance via item replacement is feasible; formal degradation analysis is underway.\"\n\n    '\n- name: item_focused_trees\n  plan_id: plan_009\n  description: 'Implement Item-Focused Trees (Tutz & Berger 2016) treating model release\n    date as continuous covariate, comparing detection power and flexibility vs categorical\n    DIF.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    n_items_analyzed: 12508\n    continuous_covariate: release_date (days since 2023-01-01)\n    ift_c_pct_equivalent: 28.4\n    ift_vs_categorical_flag_jaccard: 0.76\n    ift_unique_flags: 412\n    categorical_unique_flags: 891\n    ift_auc_semi_synthetic: 0.91\n    categorical_auc_semi_synthetic: 0.88\n    ift_smooth_transition_detected: 0.34\n    tree_depth_median: 3\n    split_points_cluster_around: 2023-Q4 and 2024-Q2\n  result_rationale: 'In best tier, Item-Focused Trees capture gradual DIF transitions\n    that categorical splits miss. IFT identifies 28.4% of items as functionally unstable\n    (vs 31.3% categorical) — slightly fewer because continuous modeling avoids the\n    artificial inflation from forcing a binary cohort split. Jaccard 0.76 with categorical\n    flags shows substantial overlap but 412 unique IFT detections capture items with\n    smooth temporal drift (34% show gradual transition patterns). AUC 0.91 on semi-synthetic\n    slightly outperforms categorical (0.88) because IFT''s flexible splits adapt to\n    the actual contamination timing. Tree split points cluster around 2023-Q4 and\n    2024-Q2, aligning with major model release waves (Llama-2→3 transition, Qwen-1.5→2).\n    This provides temporal resolution that categorical DIF cannot.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - 'Table (appendix): Item-Focused Tree vs categorical DIF detection concordance'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure (appendix): Example IFT tree for a single MMLU item showing DIF trajectory\n    over release date'\n  - 'Figure (appendix): Split-point histogram showing clustering around model release\n    waves'\n  analysis: 'P2 supplementary analysis. In best tier, IFT adds methodological sophistication\n    without changing the core finding — 28-31% of items are unstable regardless of\n    detection method. The temporal resolution (split points at model release waves)\n    provides interpretive value: instability is concentrated at model generation transitions,\n    consistent with the capability- improvement interpretation (ρ=0.307 from plan_001).\n    Appears as Appendix C, ~0.5 pages. Strengthens Claim 1 by showing the 38% finding\n    is robust to methodological variation.\n\n    '\n- name: architecture_stratified_dif\n  plan_id: plan_010\n  description: 'Run DIF within decoder-only models only to test whether architecture\n    homogeneity reduces spurious DIF and improves IRT model fit.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    n_decoder_only_total: 3847\n    n_decoder_only_ref: 892\n    n_decoder_only_foc: 541\n    decoder_only_c_pct: 22.1\n    full_population_c_pct: 31.3\n    c_pct_reduction: 29.4%\n    irt_rmsea_full: 0.082\n    irt_rmsea_decoder_only: 0.037\n    irt_cfi_decoder_only: 0.93\n    irt_tli_decoder_only: 0.91\n    irt_srmr_decoder_only: 0.028\n    semi_synthetic_delta_tpr_decoder_only: 0.72\n    semi_synthetic_delta_tpr_full: 0.674\n    semi_synthetic_fpr_decoder_only: 0.221\n    semi_synthetic_fpr_full: 0.314\n    decoder_only_sanity_random_split_c_pct: 0.12\n    dif_flag_overlap_full_vs_decoder_only: 0.68\n    enrichment_vs_li_decoder_only: 0.98\n  result_rationale: 'In best tier, restricting to decoder-only models removes architecture\n    as a confounding factor — the unidimensional IRT assumption is more tenable when\n    all models share the same basic architecture (autoregressive transformer). RMSEA\n    drops from 0.082 (poor fit) to 0.037 (acceptable fit), confirming that architecture\n    heterogeneity was driving model misfit, not the items themselves. C% drops from\n    31.3% to 22.1% — a 29% reduction — meaning ~9 pp of the original flags were architecture-confounded\n    (encoder-decoder and mixture-of-experts models process items differently, creating\n    artificial DIF). The remaining 22.1% represents genuine temporal instability within\n    a homogeneous architecture population. Semi-synthetic validation improves: FPR\n    drops from 0.314 to 0.221 (fewer false alarms), ΔTPR increases from 0.674 to 0.72\n    (better signal-to-noise). Sanity check confirms the reduction is not from power\n    loss (random split C% stays at 0.12%). Flag overlap of 0.68 with full-population\n    analysis shows the core DIF items persist — architecture stratification refines\n    rather than contradicts the original finding.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - 'Table 4: IRT fit diagnostics — full population vs decoder-only subgroup'\n  - 'Table 5: DIF detection rates and semi-synthetic validation — architecture-stratified\n    vs full'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 5: C% comparison bar chart — full population (31.3%) vs decoder-only (22.1%)\n    vs random sanity (0.12%)'\n  - 'Figure 6: IRT fit improvement — RMSEA/CFI/TLI comparison showing acceptable fit\n    in homogeneous subgroup'\n  analysis: 'CRITICAL experiment for best tier — transforms the strongest reviewer\n    objection into a methodological contribution. The finding is dual: (1) architecture\n    heterogeneity inflates DIF by ~9 pp, confirming the concern is real; (2) 22.1%\n    genuine instability persists even in a homogeneous population, confirming the\n    core finding is NOT an artifact. The practical recommendation — \"run DIF within\n    architecture-homogeneous subpopulations\" — is simple, zero-cost, and immediately\n    actionable. This appears as §4.3 in the main text (~1.5 pages) and is referenced\n    in the abstract. In best tier, reviewers read this as: \"the authors anticipated\n    our concern, ran the experiment, showed the method is refinable, and the core\n    finding survives.\" This is the difference between a borderline accept and a clear\n    accept. IRT RMSEA 0.037 falls within the best-tier target range [0.02, 0.05],\n    and C% 22.1% is within [18, 25]. The architecture stratification narrative complements\n    Growing Pains: their anchor-item approach also benefits from architecture-homogeneous\n    calibration.'\n","numLines":488,"startLine":1,"totalLines":488}}}
