(Lord's chi-square, Raju), PSN-IRT (Polo et al. AAAI 2026), Wang et al. 2026 on RL contamination fragility, Tao et al. 2026\n3. Semi-synthetic framework limited by simplified injection mechanism\n4. 38% instability may be \"mostly capability improvement\" (ρ=0.307), undermining the \"comparability concern\" framing\n5. RL robustness overclaimed (N=13, group-level vs individual-level comparison is apples-to-oranges)\n6. Cumulative Bayesian DIF is entirely simulated, feels speculative\n7. Growing Pains comparison not empirically tested (no C% among anchor items)\n8. ConStat comparison unfair (different detection tasks)\n9. Paper tries to cover too many findings, giving each insufficient depth\n\n**R2 Scores**: Novelty 2/4, Significance 3/4, Clarity 3/4\n\n## Your Task\n\nRespond concisely (under 400 words):\n1. Which of R2's points do you **agree** with?\n2. Which do you **disagree** with or see differently?\n3. Has R2's review changed your overall assessment? Would you revise your score?\n","uuid":"af8de08e-f8d3-439a-977b-6495f53cf192","session_id":"bed74537-8027-4ea2-ace4-14fc8a5d1a29"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) in a roundtable discussion. You reviewed this paper focusing on **methods and experiments**. Now read Reviewer 2's review (focused on novelty and related work) and respond with agreements, disagreements, and any updated views.\n\n## Your Original R1 Review Summary\n\nYou scored the paper **4/6** (Weak Accept). Key points:\n- MH-DIF application is technically correct, semi-synthetic framework is well-designed\n- Thorough sanity checks and honest negative results are above average\n- Main concerns: uncalibrated ETS thresholds for LLM populations (no sensitivity analysis across |Δ_MH| thresholds), restriction to uniform DIF only, missing multiple testing discussion, underpowered RL analysis (N=13), limited cross-benchmark (only MMLU+GSM8K)\n- The 31-38% baseline FPR is uncomfortably high and the paper doesn't fully explain it\n- Semi-synthetic injection (flipping to correct) may not match real contamination patterns\n- Missing experiments: threshold sensitivity, non-uniform DIF, alternative injection mechanisms, model quality filtering, temporal boundary sensitivity, more benchmarks\n- Soundness: 3/4, Empirical Rigor: 3/4\n\n## R2's Review (Novelty & Related Work)\n\nR2 also scored **4/6**. Key points:\n\n**R2's Strengths**: Scale is novel (5,227×12,508 unprecedented), ground truth meta-critique is strongest novelty contribution, cross-subject matching is clean innovation, \"DIF as diagnostic lens\" well-supported by two independent evidence lines, honest framing\n\n**R2's Weaknesses**:\n1. Core method is off-the-shelf MH-DIF (40-year-old technique) with no algorithmic novelty\n2. Missing related work: measurement invariance (Meredith 1993), MIMIC models (Muthen 1985, Woods 2009), propensity score matching for DIF (Teresi 2007), IRT-based DIF methods (Lord's chi-square, Raju), PSN-IRT (Polo et al. AAAI 2026), Wang et al. 2026 on RL contamination fragility, Tao et al. 2026\n3. Semi-synthetic framework limited by simplified injection mechanism\n4. 38% instability may be \"mostly capability improvement\" (ρ=0.307), undermining the \"comparability concern\" framing\n5. RL robustness overclaimed (N=13, group-level vs individual-level comparison is apples-to-oranges)\n6. Cumulative Bayesian DIF is entirely simulated, feels speculative\n7. Growing Pains comparison not empirically tested (no C% among anchor items)\n8. ConStat comparison unfair (different detection tasks)\n9. Paper tries to cover too many findings, giving each insufficient depth\n\n**R2 Scores**: Novelty 2/4, Significance 3/4, Clarity 3/4\n\n## Your Task\n\nRespond concisely (under 400 words):\n1. Which of R2's points do you **agree** with?\n2. Which do you **disagree** with or see differently?\n3. Has R2's review changed your overall assessment? Would you revise your score?\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01WBi6WFvsigjGtVCqfcZCAu","session_id":"bed74537-8027-4ea2-ace4-14fc8a5d1a29","uuid":"ff2fef28-6361-45dc-a09d-857d17820464","timestamp":"2026-05-21T02:03:16.809Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01NkGE11rbFhV8qNy8nUfFWN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WP56cGgVnFKRJkq7X1udYJ","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2 responds to R1's review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) in a roundtable discussion. You reviewed this paper focusing on **novelty and related work**. Now read Reviewer 1's review (focused on methods and experiments) and respond with agreements, disagreements, and any updated views.\n\n## Your Original R2 Review Summary\n\nYou scored the paper **4/6** (Weak Accept). Key points:\n- Novelty is mostly empirical, not algorithmic (MH-DIF is off-the-shelf)\n- Ground truth meta-critique is strongest novelty contribution\n- Missing many important related works (measurement invariance, MIMIC, propensity score matching, IRT-based DIF, PSN-IRT, concurrent RL contamination works)\n- 38% instability may be largely capability improvement (ρ=0.307)\n- RL robustness overclaimed (N=13), Bayesian DIF speculative\n- Paper tries to cover too much; too many findings at expense of depth\n- Novelty 2/4, Significance 3/4, Clarity 3/4\n\n## R1's Review (Methods & Experiments)\n\nR1 also scored **4/6**. Key points:\n\n**R1's Strengths**: Experimental chain is exceptionally systematic (11+ experiments, three structural remediation attempts before pivoting). Semi-synthetic framework well-designed with dose-response curve. Cross-subject matching (ΔFPR=0.000) clean contribution. Sanity checks thorough (random split 0.18%, architecture random 0.12%). GSM1K validation well-executed with leave-one-out robustness. Downstream impact analysis (z=-8 to -49σ) informative and counterintuitive. Honest framing strengthens paper.\n\n**R1's Weaknesses**:\n1. Baseline FPR 31-38% uncomfortably high and unexplained — cannot distinguish genuine capability improvement from instability\n2. ETS thresholds uncalibrated for LLM setting — no sensitivity analysis across |Δ_MH| thresholds, 38% headline may change substantially at threshold 2.0\n3. Multiple testing correction not addressed for 12,508 simultaneous tests\n4. Only uniform DIF detected; non-uniform DIF (varying by ability level) ignored; no comparison with logistic regression DIF or SIBTEST\n5. Semi-synthetic injection (flipping to correct) is oversimplified vs real contamination\n6. Cumulative Bayesian DIF lacks methodological detail (prior, batch ordering sensitivity)\n7. Bifactor TLI=0.89 below conventional 0.90 threshold; equating reduces C% by only 2.4pp\n8. RL robustness severely underpowered (N=13)\n9. GSM1K validation N=33, wide confidence interval\n10. Missing experiments: threshold sensitivity, non-uniform DIF, alternative injection mechanisms, model quality filtering, temporal boundary sensitivity, more benchmarks\n\n**R1 Scores**: Soundness 3/4, Empirical Rigor 3/4\n\n## Your Task\n\nRespond concisely (under 400 words):\n1. Which of R1's points do you **agree** with?\n2. Which do you **disagree** with or see differently?\n3. Has R1's review changed your overall assessment? Would you revise your score?\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3757,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8256,"cache_read_input_tokens":46139,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8256},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bed74537-8027-4ea2-ace4-14fc8a5d1a29","uuid":"1b302ee1-c208-44c8-8736-417b39a85f0a"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a7f59ad55d13f83b7","tool_use_id":"toolu_01WP56cGgVnFKRJkq7X1udYJ","description":"R2 responds to R1's review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) in a roundtable discussion. You reviewed this paper focusing on **novelty and related work**. Now read Reviewer 1's review (focused on methods and experiments) and respond with agreements, disagreements, and any updated views.\n\n## Your Original R2 Review Summary\n\nYou scored the paper **4/6** (Weak Accept). Key points:\n- Novelty is mostly empirical, not algorithmic (MH-DIF is off-the-shelf)\n- Ground truth meta-critique is strongest novelty contribution\n- Missing many important related works (measurement invariance, MIMIC, propensity score matching, IRT-based DIF, PSN-IRT, concurrent RL contamination works)\n- 38% instability may be largely capability improvement (ρ=0.307)\n- RL robustness overclaimed (N=13), Bayesian DIF speculative\n- Paper tries to cover too much; too many findings at expense of depth\n- Novelty 2/4, Significance 3/4, Clarity 3/4\n\n## R1's Review (Methods & Experiments)\n\nR1 also scored **4/6**. Key points:\n\n**R1's Strengths**: Experimental chain is exceptionally systematic (11+ experiments, three structural remediation attempts before pivoting). Semi-synthetic framework well-designed with dose-response curve. Cross-subject matching (ΔFPR=0.000) clean contribution. Sanity checks thorough (random split 0.18%, architecture random 0.12%). GSM1K validation well-executed with leave-one-out robustness. Downstream impact analysis (z=-8 to -49σ) informative and counterintuitive. Honest framing strengthens paper.\n\n**R1's Weaknesses**:\n1. Baseline FPR 31-38% uncomfortably high and unexplained — cannot distinguish genuine capability improvement from instability\n2. ETS thresholds uncalibrated for LLM setting — no sensitivity analysis across |Δ_MH| thresholds, 38% headline may change substantially at threshold 2.0\n3. Multiple testing correction not addressed for 12,508 simultaneous tests\n4. Only uniform DIF detected; non-uniform DIF (varying by ability level) ignored; no comparison with logistic regression DIF or SIBTEST\n5. Semi-synthetic injection (flipping to correct) is oversimplified vs real contamination\n6. Cumulative Bayesian DIF lacks methodological detail (prior, batch ordering sensitivity)\n7. Bifactor TLI=0.89 below conventional 0.90 threshold; equating reduces C% by only 2.4pp\n8. RL robustness severely underpowered (N=13)\n9. GSM1K validation N=33, wide confidence interval\n10. Missing experiments: threshold sensitivity, non-uniform DIF, alternative injection mechanisms, model quality filtering, temporal boundary sensitivity, more benchmarks\n\n**R1 Scores**: Soundness 3/4, Empirical Rigor 3/4\n\n## Your Task\n\nRespond concisely (under 400 words):\n1. Which of R1's points do you **agree** with?\n2. Which do you **disagree** with or see differently?\n3. Has R1's review changed your overall assessment? Would you revise your score?\n","uuid":"46887933-736e-4d39-82af-43f05354b7cb","session_id":"bed74537-8027-4ea2-ace4-14fc8a5d1a29"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) in a roundtable discussion. You reviewed this paper focusing on **novelty and related work**. Now read Reviewer 1's review (focused on methods and experiments) and respond with agreements, disagreements, and any updated views.\n\n## Your Original R2 Review Summary\n\nYou scored the paper **4/6** (Weak Accept). Key points:\n- Novelty is mostly empirical, not algorithmic (MH-DIF is off-the-shelf)\n- Ground truth meta-critique is strongest novelty contribution\n- Missing many important related works (measurement invariance, MIMIC, propensity score matching, IRT-based DIF, PSN-IRT, concurrent RL contamination works)\n- 38% instability may be largely capability improvement (ρ=0.307)\n- RL robustness overclaimed (N=13), Bayesian DIF speculative\n- Paper tries to cover too much; too many findings at expense of depth\n- Novelty 2/4, Significance 3/4, Clarity 3/4\n\n## R1's Review (Methods & Experiments)\n\nR1 also scored **4/6**. Key points:\n\n**R1's Strengths**: Experimental chain is exceptionally systematic (11+ experiments, three structural remediation attempts before pivoting). Semi-synthetic framework well-designed with dose-response curve. Cross-subject matching (ΔFPR=0.000) clean contribution. Sanity checks thorough (random split 0.18%, architecture random 0.12%). GSM1K validation well-executed with leave-one-out robustness. Downstream impact analysis (z=-8 to -49σ) informative and counterintuitive. Honest framing strengthens paper.\n\n**R1's Weaknesses**:\n1. Baseline FPR 31-38% uncomfortably high and unexplained — cannot distinguish genuine capability improvement from instability\n2. ETS thresholds uncalibrated for LLM setting — no sensitivity analysis across |Δ_MH| thresholds, 38% headline may change substantially at threshold 2.0\n3. Multiple testing correction not addressed for 12,508 simultaneous tests\n4. Only uniform DIF detected; non-uniform DIF (varying by ability level) ignored; no comparison with logistic regression DIF or SIBTEST\n5. Semi-synthetic injection (flipping to correct) is oversimplified vs real contamination\n6. Cumulative Bayesian DIF lacks methodological detail (prior, batch ordering sensitivity)\n7. Bifactor TLI=0.89 below conventional 0.90 threshold; equating reduces C% by only 2.4pp\n8. RL robustness severely underpowered (N=13)\n9. GSM1K validation N=33, wide confidence interval\n10. Missing experiments: threshold sensitivity, non-uniform DIF, alternative injection mechanisms, model quality filtering, temporal boundary sensitivity, more benchmarks\n\n**R1 Scores**: Soundness 3/4, Empirical Rigor 3/4\n\n## Your Task\n\nRespond concisely (under 400 words):\n1. Which of R1's points do you **agree** with?\n2. Which do you **disagree** with or see differently?\n3. Has R1's review changed your overall assessment? Would you revise your score?\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01WP56cGgVnFKRJkq7X1udYJ","session_id":"bed74537-8027-4ea2-ace4-14fc8a5d1a29","uuid":"e144c95b-96a6-4587-b0cc-a8b4a46f1f9a","timestamp":"2026-05-21T02:03:31.714Z"}
