{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012VhTfN4p3SrXDfhUW6qcUm","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/report.md\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"# Handover — 2026-05-21\\\\n\\\\n> 由 Agent 生成，每次状态变更后自动更新。硬限 150 行。\\\\n\\\\n## 运行中\\\\n（无）\\\\n\\\\n## 待处理\\\\n- plan_015_bifactor_irt: Bifactor IRT（1 general + 57 subject-specific factors）的 general θ 分层能将 temporal cohort baseline FPR 从 31% 降至 <15%，同时在 semi-synthetic 框架下保持 TPR>0.60 at p=0.5。Bifactor 通过分离学科特异性能力和通用能力，消除 subject-level 能力差异对 MH-DIF 的混淆。（准备中）\\\\n\\\\n## 近期结论\\\\n- **plan_004_gsm8k_cross_benchmark** (negative): GSM8K 的 temporal DIF (2023 vs 2024) 将展示与 MMLU 类似的 30-40% ETS C%，证明 temporal DIF 现象跨 benchmark 泛化。使用 METABENCH 其他 benchmarks 总分做 external matching（GSM8K 无 subject 分层），预期 baseline FPR 更可控。如果 C% 在同一量级 → DIF 作为 benchmark health diagnostic 的泛化性得到验证。 ← 基于 plan_001_real_data_dif_mmlu\\\\n- **plan_017_downstream_impact** (negative): 移除 temporal DIF-flagged items (ETS C, ~31% of MMLU items) 后，模型跨代排名更稳定（Spearman ρ 提升），且稳定性提升显著优于随机移除相同数量 items 的 baseline。这证明 DIF 不只标记 noise，而是精确识别了 problematic items，为 DIF 作为 benchmark quality tool 提供 downstream evidence。 ← 基于 plan_001_real_data_dif_mmlu\\\\n- **plan_016_within_subject_semi_synthetic** (success): 在 subject 内部使用 within-subject total score 做 MH 分层的 semi-synthetic injection 中，DIF 仍能检测差异性污染（ΔTPR > 0.3 at p=0.5）。这证明 DIF 方法在 subject 级别也有效，plan_014 的 38% within-subject C% 反映的是真实代际能力差异而非方法失败。 ← 基于 plan_014_within_subject_dif\\\\n  [plan_016_report](/api/workspace-files/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/plan_016_within_subject_semi_synthetic/report/plan_016_report.md) | [plan_016_dose_response](/api/workspace-files/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/plan_016_within_subject_semi_synthetic/data/within_subject_dose_response.csv) | [plan_016_revised_dose_response](/api/workspace-files/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/plan_016_within_subject_semi_synthetic/data/within_subject_dose_response_e6d86f.csv) | [plan_016_revised_report](/api/workspace-files/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/plan_016_within_subject_semi_synthetic/report/plan_016_report_fb64fc.md)\\\\n\\\\n## 趋势\\\\n成功 5 / 负向 6 / 失败 0 / 共 11\\\\n\\\\n## 下一步\\\\n（无活跃 idea，请通过 ideas.md 添加）\\\\n\\\\n## 资源状态\\\\n（无服务器配置）\\\\n\\\\n## 告警\\\\n（无）\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 2009, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01WP56cGgVnFKRJkq7X1udYJ","session_id":"bed74537-8027-4ea2-ace4-14fc8a5d1a29","uuid":"4736679f-34c2-4d2b-b65d-20c0051bb99c","timestamp":"2026-05-21T02:03:39.165Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"ae865cd5e10b38923","tool_use_id":"toolu_01WBi6WFvsigjGtVCqfcZCAu","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R1 responds to R2's review","usage":{"total_tokens":14977,"tool_uses":0,"duration_ms":22465},"uuid":"34ac701a-ceda-43b5-925d-4dac04233196","session_id":"bed74537-8027-4ea2-ace4-14fc8a5d1a29"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WBi6WFvsigjGtVCqfcZCAu","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## R1 Response to R2's Review\n\n### Agreements\n\nR2's most important observation is one I underweighted: the 38% instability being largely explained by capability improvement (ρ=0.307) genuinely undermines the paper's central framing. If most \"unstable\" items are simply harder items that newer models solve through genuine capability gains, the comparability concern shrinks considerably. I should have pressed harder on this in my original review.\n\nI fully agree on the RL robustness overclaim (N=13). I flagged this as underpowered, but R2's additional point about group-level vs. individual-level comparison being apples-to-oranges is sharper and well-taken. The paper cannot conclude RL \"preserves contamination signals\" from this design.\n\nThe missing related work critique is devastating and correct. I focused on methodological execution and neglected to check whether the paper adequately situated itself. The omission of IRT-based DIF alternatives (Lord's chi-square, Raju's area method), MIMIC models, and measurement invariance literature is a serious gap for a paper claiming to bridge psychometrics and LLM evaluation. The PSN-IRT (Polo et al. 2026) omission is particularly glaring given direct topical overlap.\n\nI also agree the Cumulative Bayesian DIF section feels speculative and underdeveloped -- it reads like a separate short paper stapled on.\n\n### Disagreements\n\nR2 characterizes MH-DIF as \"off-the-shelf with no algorithmic novelty\" and scores Novelty 2/4. I think this is slightly harsh. The contribution is not algorithmic but methodological: the semi-synthetic ground truth framework, cross-subject anchor matching, and the diagnostic lens applied at scale (5,227 × 12,508) represent genuine design novelty even if the statistical test is classical. Novelty in application framing is legitimate, though the paper should be more upfront about it.\n\nI also partially disagree on the Growing Pains comparison criticism. R2 wants empirical C% testing among anchor items, but the paper's contribution is the conceptual comparison. Not every related method needs head-to-head benchmarking, though acknowledging the limitation would be appropriate.\n\nThe \"paper tries to cover too many findings\" critique is fair but I see it differently -- the breadth is partly what makes the honest-negative-results story compelling. The problem is depth per finding, not the number of findings.\n\n### Updated Assessment\n\nR2's review has shifted my view downward. The related work gaps and the ρ=0.307 reinterpretation are substantive issues I missed. I would revise my score from **4/6 to 3/6** (Borderline Reject). The execution remains competent, but the novelty deficit and the undermined central narrative together weaken the case for acceptance. A revision addressing the missing literature, separating capability gains from instability, and deepening the RL analysis could bring it back to a 4."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ae865cd5e10b38923 (use SendMessage with to: 'ae865cd5e10b38923' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 15659\ntool_uses: 0\nduration_ms: 22467</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bed74537-8027-4ea2-ace4-14fc8a5d1a29","uuid":"7c2d572b-23a3-4154-b051-2b79c4d42c70","timestamp":"2026-05-21T02:03:39.276Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) in a roundtable discussion. You reviewed this paper focusing on **methods and experiments**. Now read Reviewer 2's review (focused on novelty and related work) and respond with agreements, disagreements, and any updated views.\n\n## Your Original R1 Review Summary\n\nYou scored the paper **4/6** (Weak Accept). Key points:\n- MH-DIF application is technically correct, semi-synthetic framework is well-designed\n- Thorough sanity checks and honest negative results are above average\n- Main concerns: uncalibrated ETS thresholds for LLM populations (no sensitivity analysis across |Δ_MH| thresholds), restriction to uniform DIF only, missing multiple testing discussion, underpowered RL analysis (N=13), limited cross-benchmark (only MMLU+GSM8K)\n- The 31-38% baseline FPR is uncomfortably high and the paper doesn't fully explain it\n- Semi-synthetic injection (flipping to correct) may not match real contamination patterns\n- Missing experiments: threshold sensitivity, non-uniform DIF, alternative injection mechanisms, model quality filtering, temporal boundary sensitivity, more benchmarks\n- Soundness: 3/4, Empirical Rigor: 3/4\n\n## R2's Review (Novelty & Related Work)\n\nR2 also scored **4/6**. Key points:\n\n**R2's Strengths**: Scale is novel (5,227×12,508 unprecedented), ground truth meta-critique is strongest novelty contribution, cross-subject matching is clean innovation, \"DIF as diagnostic lens\" well-supported by two independent evidence lines, honest framing\n\n**R2's Weaknesses**:\n1. Core method is off-the-shelf MH-DIF (40-year-old technique) with no algorithmic novelty\n2. Missing related work: measurement invariance (Meredith 1993), MIMIC models (Muthen 1985, Woods 2009), propensity score matching for DIF (Teresi 2007), IRT-based DIF methods (Lord's chi-square, Raju), PSN-IRT (Polo et al. AAAI 2026), Wang et al. 2026 on RL contamination fragility, Tao et al. 2026\n3. Semi-synthetic framework limited by simplified injection mechanism\n4. 38% instability may be \"mostly capability improvement\" (ρ=0.307), undermining the \"comparability concern\" framing\n5. RL robustness overclaimed (N=13, group-level vs individual-level comparison is apples-to-oranges)\n6. Cumulative Bayesian DIF is entirely simulated, feels speculative\n7. Growing Pains comparison not empirically tested (no C% among anchor items)\n8. ConStat comparison unfair (different detection tasks)\n9. Paper tries to cover too many findings, giving each insufficient depth\n\n**R2 Scores**: Novelty 2/4, Significance 3/4, Clarity 3/4\n\n## Your Task\n\nRespond concisely (under 400 words):\n1. Which of R2's points do you **agree** with?\n2. Which do you **disagree** with or see differently?\n3. Has R2's review changed your overall assessment? Would you revise your score?\n","agentId":"ae865cd5e10b38923","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## R1 Response to R2's Review\n\n### Agreements\n\nR2's most important observation is one I underweighted: the 38% instability being largely explained by capability improvement (ρ=0.307) genuinely undermines the paper's central framing. If most \"unstable\" items are simply harder items that newer models solve through genuine capability gains, the comparability concern shrinks considerably. I should have pressed harder on this in my original review.\n\nI fully agree on the RL robustness overclaim (N=13). I flagged this as underpowered, but R2's additional point about group-level vs. individual-level comparison being apples-to-oranges is sharper and well-taken. The paper cannot conclude RL \"preserves contamination signals\" from this design.\n\nThe missing related work critique is devastating and correct. I focused on methodological execution and neglected to check whether the paper adequately situated itself. The omission of IRT-based DIF alternatives (Lord's chi-square, Raju's area method), MIMIC models, and measurement invariance literature is a serious gap for a paper claiming to bridge psychometrics and LLM evaluation. The PSN-IRT (Polo et al. 2026) omission is particularly glaring given direct topical overlap.\n\nI also agree the Cumulative Bayesian DIF section feels speculative and underdeveloped -- it reads like a separate short paper stapled on.\n\n### Disagreements\n\nR2 characterizes MH-DIF as \"off-the-shelf with no algorithmic novelty\" and scores Novelty 2/4. I think this is slightly harsh. The contribution is not algorithmic but methodological: the semi-synthetic ground truth framework, cross-subject anchor matching, and the diagnostic lens applied at scale (5,227 × 12,508) represent genuine design novelty even if the statistical test is classical. Novelty in application framing is legitimate, though the paper should be more upfront about it.\n\nI also partially disagree on the Growing Pains comparison criticism. R2 wants empirical C% testing among anchor items, but the paper's contribution is the conceptual comparison. Not every related method needs head-to-head benchmarking, though acknowledging the limitation would be appropriate.\n\nThe \"paper tries to cover too many findings\" critique is fair but I see it differently -- the breadth is partly what makes the honest-negative-results story compelling. The problem is depth per finding, not the number of findings.\n\n### Updated Assessment\n\nR2's review has shifted my view downward. The related work gaps and the ρ=0.307 reinterpretation are substantive issues I missed. I would revise my score from **4/6 to 3/6** (Borderline Reject). The execution remains competent, but the novelty deficit and the undermined central narrative together weaken the case for acceptance. A revision addressing the missing literature, separating capability gains from instability, and deepening the RL analysis could bring it back to a 4."}],"totalDurationMs":22467,"totalTokens":15659,"totalToolUseCount":0,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6720,"cache_read_input_tokens":8254,"output_tokens":683,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6720},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":683,"cache_read_input_tokens":8254,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6720,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6720,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}}}
