{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01EELF2zJxfKnQSWsYWqLKQ8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BAvbsnLh5dzXRrxXG2dNuy","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Dream Review Report: Psychometric Characterization of Item Instability in LLM Benchmarks\n\n**Paper**: Psychometric Characterization of Item Instability in LLM Benchmarks: A Large-Scale Differential Item Functioning Analysis of MMLU  \n**Review mode**: Full (2-reviewer roundtable)  \n**Date**: 2026-05-21  \n**Venue standard**: Top ML venue, ~25% acceptance, 6-point scale\n\n---\n\n## Roundtable Summary\n\nTwo reviewers independently scored the paper **4/6** (Weak Accept). After cross-sharing and discussion, R1 revised down to **3/6** (Weak Reject) citing novelty gaps and the undermined central narrative (ρ=0.307 suggests instability is partly capability improvement). R2 held at **4/6** but acknowledged the paper needs major revision. The consensus is **Borderline Reject (3–4/6)** — the paper addresses an important problem with commendable experimental rigor and intellectual honesty, but methodological gaps, limited novelty, and an underdeveloped central narrative prevent acceptance in current form.\n\n---\n\n## Final Scores\n\n| Dimension | R1 | R2 | Consensus |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Soundness | 3/4 | — | 3/4 |\n| Empirical Rigor | 3/4 | — | 3/4 |\n| Novelty | — | 2/4 | 2/4 |\n| Significance | — | 3/4 | 3/4 |\n| Clarity | — | 3/4 | 3/4 |\n| **Overall** | **3/6** | **4/6** | **3.5/6 (Borderline Reject)** |\n| Confidence | 4/5 | 4/5 | 4/5 |\n\n---\n\n## Strengths (Consensus)\n\n1. **Scale and ambition.** The largest psychometric analysis of an LLM benchmark to date (5,227 models × 12,508 items). The transfer of DIF methodology to this domain is non-trivial and the execution is systematic.\n\n2. **Ground truth meta-critique (Claim 2).** The observation that web-overlap contamination labels measure global exposure, not differential functioning, is the paper's strongest conceptual contribution. It affects the validation methodology of Min-K%, ConStat, DVD, and all work using web-overlap as item-level ground truth. Both reviewers consider this independently valuable.\n\n3. **Semi-synthetic validation framework (Claim 3).** The cross-subject matching innovation (ΔFPR = 0.000) is a clean, practical methodological contribution. The insight that matching on within-subject total score contaminates the matching variable is subtle and empirically well-demonstrated.\n\n4. **Thorough controls and honest reporting.** Random-split sanity checks (C% = 0.18% vs. temporal 31.3%), architecture-stratified analysis (C% drops to 22.1% with improved IRT fit), dose-response curves, permutation tests, and leave-one-out robustness analyses. The transparent treatment of negative results (three structural corrections failed) is above average for the venue.\n\n5. **DIF as diagnostic lens (Claim 4).** Supported by two independent evidence lines: (a) DIF item removal disrupts rankings more than random removal (z = −8 to −49σ), and (b) DIF flags anti-correlate with GSM1K contamination gaps (r = −0.478, p = 0.005). This reframing from \"find bad items\" to \"understand what changed\" is well-argued.\n\n6. **Architecture stratification (§4.3).** Transforms a potential fatal objection into a practical recommendation. The 29% C% reduction with dramatically improved IRT fit (RMSEA 0.082 → 0.037) is the paper's most methodologically satisfying experiment.\n\n---\n\n## Weaknesses (Consensus)\n\n### Critical Issues\n\n1. **ETS threshold uncalibrated for LLM populations (both reviewers).** The |Δ_MH| ≥ 1.5 threshold for ETS C classification is borrowed directly from human educational testing without calibration or sensitivity analysis. The 38% headline number is anchored to this arbitrary cutoff. If the threshold were 2.0, the finding would change substantially. **The paper must report C% at multiple thresholds (e.g., 1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5) to demonstrate qualitative robustness.** This is the single most important missing analysis.\n\n2. **ρ = 0.307 undermines the central narrative (both reviewers).** The correlation between C% and subject-level accuracy improvement suggests a substantial fraction of \"instability\" is models genuinely getting better at specific domains. The paper frames this honestly but does not adequately decompose capability-improvement DIF from genuine measurement anomaly DIF. A partial correlation or regression decomposition is needed. Without it, a reviewer can reasonably ask: if \"instability\" is mostly \"progress,\" is this a comparability concern or just evidence that benchmarks are working?\n\n3. **Missing related work (R2, endorsed by R1).** The paper omits several directly relevant lines of work:\n   - Measurement invariance literature (Meredith 1993, Vandenberg & Lance 2000) — DIF is a special case of scalar invariance testing\n   - MIMIC models (Muthén 1985, Woods 2009) — handles continuous covariates, directly addresses θ-conditioning failure\n   - Propensity score matching for DIF (Teresi et al. 2007) — addresses impact-vs-DIF confounding\n   - IRT-based DIF methods (Lord's χ², Raju's area) — no justification for MH over these alternatives\n   - PSN-IRT (Polo et al., AAAI 2026) — concurrent work applying IRT to MMLU\n   - Wang et al. (2026) on RL contamination fragility — directly relevant to RL robustness discussion\n   - Tao et al. (2026) on detecting contamination from RL post-training\n   \n   For a paper claiming to bridge psychometrics and ML evaluation, the psychometric literature coverage is surprisingly thin.\n\n4. **No algorithmic novelty (R2, partially contested by R1).** The core method is off-the-shelf MH-DIF applied to a binary response matrix — a direct application of a 40-year-old technique. The novelty is empirical (findings) and methodological (framework design), not algorithmic. R1 argues application novelty is legitimate; R2 considers it borderline for a top venue main track. Consensus: the novelty is real but modest, and the paper should be more explicit that its contribution is empirical characterization, not methodological innovation.\n\n### Major Issues\n\n5. **Multiple testing not addressed (R1, endorsed by R2).** 12,508 simultaneous MH tests with no Bonferroni, FDR, or other correction. The ETS classification's dual requirement (effect size + significance) likely controls this, but the argument is never made explicit. This needs at minimum a paragraph of discussion.\n\n6. **Only uniform DIF detected (R1, endorsed by R2).** MH detects only constant difficulty shifts across ability levels. Non-uniform DIF — where contamination varies by model ability — is plausible (stronger models more likely to have seen specific items) and would be missed entirely. No comparison with logistic regression DIF or SIBTEST is provided.\n\n7. **RL robustness severely underpowered (both reviewers).** N = 13 reasoning-RL models is insufficient to support the \"RL robustness\" claim. The comparison with per-model detectors (Min-K%, ConStat) is apples-to-oranges: DIF operates at group level and is inherently less sensitive to individual model additions. The paper appropriately places this in the appendix but overclaims in §6 Discussion.\n\n8. **Semi-synthetic injection oversimplified (both reviewers).** Flipping incorrect to correct responses at rate p assumes contamination manifests as deterministic answer knowledge. Real contamination may involve partial knowledge, distractor elimination, or format memorization. The ΔTPR numbers may overestimate detection power against realistic contamination patterns.\n\n9. **Cumulative Bayesian DIF speculative (both reviewers).** §5.3 introduces a Bayesian DIF accumulation framework that is entirely simulated with no validation on actual METABENCH temporal data. Insufficient methodological detail (prior specification, batch ordering sensitivity, threshold sensitivity). Feels like a separate, underdeveloped contribution stapled onto the paper.\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n10. **GSM1K validation sample size.** N = 33 matched models with wide confidence interval (~[−0.70, −0.17] at 95%). The anti-correlation finding is directionally convincing but imprecise.\n\n11. **Bifactor TLI = 0.89** is below the conventional 0.90 acceptability threshold, yet equating results from this model are presented without adequate caveat.\n\n12. **Paper scope too broad.** Four claims + architecture stratification + structural constraints + Bayesian DIF + RL robustness + cross-benchmark validation + future work with pilot results (Tables 7–11). Each receives insufficient depth. The Future Work section is disproportionately long with preliminary numbers that feel like padding.\n\n13. **Growing Pains comparison not empirically tested.** The paper claims DIF \"complements\" Growing Pains' anchor-item approach but does not test C% among Growing Pains' specific anchor items.\n\n---\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n1. What is C% at alternative ETS thresholds (|Δ_MH| ≥ 1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5)? Does the qualitative finding (>20% instability) survive across thresholds?\n\n2. If you partial out accuracy improvement from C%, what fraction of the 38% instability remains unexplained by capability gains?\n\n3. Why MH-DIF specifically rather than logistic regression DIF, which naturally accommodates continuous covariates and would have avoided the θ-conditioning failure?\n\n4. For cross-subject matching ΔFPR = 0.000: is this exactly zero or rounded? Across how many injection levels and seeds?\n\n5. What drives the 9.2pp C% reduction in architecture stratification — removal of non-decoder models, or the smaller sample size (3,847 vs. 5,227)?\n\n6. What is the C% among Growing Pains' specific anchor items?\n\n7. For Cumulative Bayesian DIF: how sensitive is TPR/FPR to the Bayes factor threshold (2.0 vs. 3.2 vs. 5.0)?\n\n8. How does restricting to models above a minimum accuracy threshold affect C%? Very weak models (near-random) may inflate DIF statistics.\n\n---\n\n## Experiment Coverage Assessment\n\n**Executed and included in paper:**\n- Core temporal DIF analysis — ✅ Main text §4.1\n- Architecture-stratified DIF (plan_010) — ✅ Main text §4.3, strong\n- Semi-synthetic validation — ✅ Main text §4.4\n- Structural constraints / bifactor (plan_005 partial) — ✅ §4.5\n- Benchmark redundancy / diagnostic lens (plan_017) — ✅ §5.1\n- Cross-benchmark GSM8K validation — ✅ §5.2\n- Cumulative Bayesian DIF (plan_007) — ✅ §5.3, underdeveloped\n- RL robustness (plan_002) — ✅ Appendix A, underpowered (N=13)\n- Item-Focused Trees (plan_009) — ✅ Appendix B\n\n**Dropped but pilot results reported in Future Work:**\n- ConStat (plan_003a) — Table 8\n- DVD (plan_003b) — Table 9\n- Ensemble fusion (plan_003c) — Table 7\n- Item generation (plan_006) — Table 11\n- Graceful degradation (plan_008) — Table 10\n\nThe paper covers the core experiments adequately. The dropped experiments are appropriately positioned as future work. The main coverage gap is not missing experiments but missing **sensitivity analyses** on the experiments that were run (threshold sensitivity, temporal boundary sensitivity, model quality filtering).\n\n---\n\n## Recommendations for Revision\n\n**Must-fix for resubmission:**\n1. Add ETS threshold sensitivity analysis (C% at multiple cutoffs)\n2. Decompose instability: partial out capability improvement from C%\n3. Expand related work with measurement invariance, MIMIC, IRT-based DIF alternatives\n4. Add multiple testing discussion\n5. Either strengthen RL robustness with more models or downgrade claims to \"preliminary observation\"\n\n**Strongly recommended:**\n6. Include at least one non-uniform DIF method comparison (logistic regression DIF)\n7. Tighten scope: move Bayesian DIF and some Future Work tables to supplementary\n8. Test C% among Growing Pains anchor items\n9. Add one more benchmark beyond MMLU and GSM8K\n\n**If space permits:**\n10. Discuss alternative injection mechanisms and their implications for ΔTPR estimates\n11. Report model quality filtering sensitivity\n12. Report temporal boundary sensitivity\n\n---\n\n## Final Verdict\n\n**Borderline Reject (3.5/6).** The paper tackles an important problem — whether LLM benchmark scores remain comparable across model generations — and brings rigorous psychometric methodology to bear on it at unprecedented scale. The ground truth meta-critique, cross-subject matching innovation, and architecture stratification analysis are genuine contributions. However, the 38% headline finding rests on an uncalibrated threshold with no sensitivity analysis, the central narrative is partially undermined by the capability-improvement correlation (ρ = 0.307), the related work has significant gaps, and the paper overextends by trying to present too many results without sufficient depth on any. With a focused revision addressing threshold sensitivity, instability decomposition, and related work coverage, this could become a solid contribution. In current form, the methodological gaps and limited novelty place it below the acceptance threshold for a top ML venue.","project":"irt_equated_bench_regen","tier":"best","dream_id":"20260521T012912","overall_score":"borderline reject","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":713,"cache_read_input_tokens":62379,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":713},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bed74537-8027-4ea2-ace4-14fc8a5d1a29","uuid":"6fdebe8a-e96c-4bd9-95dc-885f363275d7"}
